    "seniorAnswer": [
      "HotSpot does NOT actually perform true stack allocation of objects. Instead, it uses 'Scalar Replacement': if an object is proven `NoEscape`, the JIT compiler decomposes the object into its individual scalar fields (primitives and references) and stores them directly in CPU registers or stack frames as local variables. The object abstraction and its 16-byte header are completely erased; zero heap allocation occurs."
    ],
    "codeSnippet": "public int getSum() {\n    Point p = new Point(10, 20); // Point object is NOT allocated on heap or stack\n    return p.x + p.y;\n}\n\n// C2 Compiler translates getSum() directly to:\n// int p_x = 10;\n// int p_y = 20;\n// return p_x + p_y; // Pure CPU register addition! Zero memory allocation!",
